---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const faq = [
	{
		q: 'How do you play chords on Gesture Synth?',
		a: 'Your left hand picks the scale degree and major or minor with a wrist tilt. Your right hand picks the chord quality by how many fingers you raise. Matching both hands to the chart plays the chord.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What do the left-hand gestures mean?',
		a: 'Raise 1–5 fingers for scale degrees I–V. Index + pinky (with the middle and ring fingers down) makes VI, and adding your thumb makes VII. Tilting your wrist flips between major and minor.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How do I change the chord quality on Gesture Synth?',
		a: 'Raise 1–4 fingers on your right hand. One finger is a plain major or minor triad, two is a first inversion, three is a seventh chord, and four is a dominant seventh (major key) or diminished seventh (minor key).',
	},
	{
		q: 'What does the right-hand thumb do?',
		a: 'Extending your right thumb drops the chord down an octave; folding it back plays the base register.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Can I practice a whole song?',
		a: 'Yes. The songbook turns each chord chart into a guided practice player with a backing pad and metronome. Pick a song, match both hands to each target shape, and the song rings out.',
	},
];
---

<Layout
	title="How to Play Gesture Synth: Hand Gestures & Chords Guide | Gesture Synth School"
	description="Learn how to play Gesture Synth with your hands. A complete guide to the hand gestures: scale degrees, major/minor tilt, chord qualities, octave, volume and tone."
	ogType="article"
	jsonLd={[
		{
			'@context': 'https://schema.org',
			'@type': 'HowTo',
			name: 'How to play Gesture Synth',
			description:
				'Learn the Gesture Synth hand gestures: left hand picks the scale degree and major/minor, right hand picks chord quality and octave.',
			totalTime: 'PT15M',
			step: [
				{
					'@type': 'HowToStep',
					position: 1,
					name: 'Turn on the camera and audio',
					text: 'Open Gesture Synth, allow camera access, and face one palm toward the webcam until the status reads "Raise a hand".',
				},
				{
					'@type': 'HowToStep',
					position: 2,
					name: 'Pick a scale degree with your left hand',
					text: 'Raise 1–5 fingers for degrees I–V. Index + pinky makes VI; add the thumb for VII.',
				},
				{
					'@type': 'HowToStep',
					position: 3,
					name: 'Flip major and minor with a wrist tilt',
					text: 'Tilt your left wrist to switch between the major and minor chord world.',
				},
				{
					'@type': 'HowToStep',
					position: 4,
					name: 'Shape the chord with your right hand',
					text: 'Raise 1–4 fingers on your right hand to choose the chord quality, from plain triad to seventh chords.',
				},
				{
					'@type': 'HowToStep',
					position: 5,
					name: 'Control octave and volume',
					text: 'Extend or fold your right thumb to shift the octave; move your right wrist up or down to control volume.',
				},
				{
					'@type': 'HowToStep',
					position: 6,
					name: 'Practice a full song',
					text: 'Open the songbook, pick a song, and follow the chord chart — the backing pad and metronome keep you on beat.',
				},
			],
		},
		{
			'@context': 'https://schema.org',
			'@type': 'FAQPage',
			mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
				'@type': 'Question',
				name: f.q,
				acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
			})),
		},
	]}
>
	<article class="content container">
		<p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
		<h1>How to Play Gesture Synth</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			Gesture Synth is a hand gesture synth: your webcam turns into a musical instrument. No
			hardware, no downloads — your hands pick the chords. Here is every gesture, verified
			against the open-source engine.
		</p>

		<h2>Learning from the community</h2>
		<p>
			Gesture Synth went viral on short video, and the community is the fastest way to learn.
			Watch how other players use the hand gestures, then practice the same songs here:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><a href="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbH1BACxNCG/">indecisive.eric&rsquo;s gesture synth tutorial</a> on Instagram</li>
			<li><a href="https://www.youtube.com/shorts/desGIrzWLIk">Rafferty Coope playing &ldquo;Let It Be&rdquo;</a> on YouTube</li>
			<li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@liv.angell/video/7666235843392064781">liv.angell&rsquo;s &ldquo;Brooklyn Baby&rdquo; tutorial</a> on TikTok</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>Getting started</h2>
		<ol>
			<li>Open <a href="https://www.gesturesynth.com">gesturesynth.com</a> and allow camera access.</li>
			<li>Face one palm toward the webcam until the status reads <strong>Raise a hand</strong>.</li>
			<li>Left hand picks the chord, right hand shapes it. Both hands move at once.</li>
		</ol>

		<h2>Left hand: scale degree + major/minor</h2>
		<p>Raise fingers on your left hand to pick the scale degree (the chord&rsquo;s place in the key):</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>1–5 fingers</strong> → degrees I–V</li>
			<li><strong>Index + pinky</strong> (middle and ring down) → VI</li>
			<li><strong>Index + pinky + thumb</strong> → VII</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Tilt your wrist to flip between <strong>major</strong> and <strong>minor</strong>. In the
			mirrored selfie view, fingers leaning left means major; leaning right means minor.
		</p>

		<h2>Right hand: chord quality</h2>
		<p>Raise fingers on your right hand to shape the chord:</p>
		<table class="gesture-table">
			<thead>
				<tr><th>Fingers</th><th>Major key</th><th>Minor key</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>1</td><td>Major triad</td><td>Minor triad</td></tr>
				<tr><td>2</td><td>Major 1st inversion</td><td>Minor 1st inversion</td></tr>
				<tr><td>3</td><td>Major 7th</td><td>Minor 7th</td></tr>
				<tr><td>4</td><td>Dominant 7th</td><td>Diminished 7th</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>

		<h2>Right thumb: octave &mdash; wrist height: volume</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Thumb extended</strong> drops the chord an octave; folded plays the base register.</li>
			<li><strong>Wrist height</strong> controls volume (higher = louder).</li>
			<li><strong>Wrist tilt</strong> sweeps the filter for a brighter or darker tone.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>Practice a whole song</h2>
		<p>
			The <a href="/">songbook</a> turns every chord chart into a guided practice session: a
			backing pad and metronome keep the beat, the chart auto-advances, and your two hands are
			scored against each target shape. Start with the beginner songs, then move on.
		</p>

		<div class="card cta">
			<h3>Ready to play?</h3>
			<p class="muted">
				Pick a song from the songbook and follow the chord chart with your hands.
			</p>
			<a class="btn" href="/">Browse the songbook</a>
			<a class="btn" href="/chords/">Chord gesture reference</a>
		</div>

		<h2>FAQ</h2>
		<div class="faq">
			{faq.map((f) => (
				<details>
					<summary>{f.q}</summary>
					<p class="muted">{f.a}</p>
				</details>
			))}
		</div>
	</article>
</Layout>
